00:01:57.320 --> 00:01:58.920
but there's a lot
of growth happened.

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:00.503
There's a lot of
innovation happening.

00:02:00.503 --> 00:02:03.470
This is a very good time
to be thinking about IoT.

00:02:03.470 --> 00:02:06.590
And specifically, one of things
that we see really clearly

00:02:06.590 --> 00:02:08.840
is this transition
where a lot of companies

00:02:08.840 --> 00:02:11.870
are moving from a proof
of concept phase and pilot

00:02:11.870 --> 00:02:15.568
phase to real deployment of
IoT solutions and production.

00:02:15.568 --> 00:02:17.110
And over the next
three years, you're

00:02:17.110 --> 00:02:19.690
going to see this
clearly increase.

00:02:19.690 --> 00:02:22.830
It's just a stat that
kind of indicates that.

00:02:22.830 --> 00:02:25.160
But this growth does
beg the question.

00:02:25.160 --> 00:02:29.100
And the question I think
it begs is, so how do you

00:02:29.100 --> 00:02:31.480
be successful in this growth?

00:02:31.480 --> 00:02:33.300
How do you take advantage of it?

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:35.790
So I put together
this framework having

00:02:35.790 --> 00:02:40.530
had the benefit of being in
IoT for the past six years,

00:02:40.530 --> 00:02:43.320
developing an IoT platform
and being here with Google

00:02:43.320 --> 00:02:45.360
and many of our
customers, outlining

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:49.147
what we call the seven steps
to business success in IoT.

00:02:49.147 --> 00:02:50.730
And it's basically
a framework, or way

00:02:50.730 --> 00:02:55.110
to think about how to navigate
the daunting, sometimes,

00:02:55.110 --> 00:02:58.650
set of business considerations,
technical considerations,

00:02:58.650 --> 00:03:01.050
and even sometimes
cultural change

00:03:01.050 --> 00:03:04.260
that has to happen within a
company or a customer base

00:03:04.260 --> 00:03:06.680
in order to really
be successful in IoT.

00:03:06.680 --> 00:03:08.430
So I'm going to step
through this quickly.

00:03:08.430 --> 00:03:10.290
And there's a lot
more behind it,

00:03:10.290 --> 00:03:13.560
but let's just kind of start
at the first part, the business

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:14.450
case.

00:03:14.450 --> 00:03:16.200
So the first thing
about the business case

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:18.270
is you have to have one.

00:03:18.270 --> 00:03:22.090
I think the biggest indicator,
one of the biggest indicators

00:03:22.090 --> 00:03:24.720
of success that we've seen
in working with companies who

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:26.500
have embarked on
this IoT journey

00:03:26.500 --> 00:03:27.970
is that the companies
have answered

00:03:27.970 --> 00:03:32.410
three fundamental questions
really, really well.

00:03:32.410 --> 00:03:35.570
Number one is who
is my customer?

00:03:35.570 --> 00:03:38.570
Number two is what
is the new value

00:03:38.570 --> 00:03:43.370
that we want to deliver to
them with a connected service?

00:03:43.370 --> 00:03:48.200
And number three is how does
that new customer value then

00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:51.170
fold back into my business
and add value to my business?

00:03:51.170 --> 00:03:54.560
And ideally, some way to really
measure that and show clear

00:03:54.560 --> 00:03:55.550
ROI.

00:03:55.550 --> 00:03:59.660
Companies that
have done that work

00:03:59.660 --> 00:04:02.510
are just really set very well to
make many of the other business

00:04:02.510 --> 00:04:04.310
decisions and technical
decisions a lot

00:04:04.310 --> 00:04:05.210
more effectively.

00:04:05.210 --> 00:04:08.840
If not, we jokingly-- my team
would refer to something called

00:04:08.840 --> 00:04:11.390
the IoT cul-de-sac, which
is not a dead end road,

00:04:11.390 --> 00:04:13.730
but it's kind of like you get
down the road for a while,

00:04:13.730 --> 00:04:16.188
and you realize you haven't
really thought through your end

00:04:16.188 --> 00:04:19.160
game, and you have to turn
around and go back and start

00:04:19.160 --> 00:04:20.459
from the beginning.

00:04:20.459 --> 00:04:21.560
So number one.

00:04:21.560 --> 00:04:23.670
Number two is the
connected product itself.

00:04:23.670 --> 00:04:26.940
So this is for OEMs, who are
often the ones implementing

00:04:26.940 --> 00:04:29.375
IoT solutions, this is the
one that can sound easy

00:04:29.375 --> 00:04:30.500
because that's what you do.

00:04:30.500 --> 00:04:32.310
You make a physical product.

00:04:32.310 --> 00:04:35.990
But when a physical product
becomes part of a 24

00:04:35.990 --> 00:04:37.910
by 7 connected
service, there can

00:04:37.910 --> 00:04:39.590
be all sorts of
new considerations,

00:04:39.590 --> 00:04:40.820
as you can imagine.

00:04:40.820 --> 00:04:43.940
The question is,
with a connected car,

00:04:43.940 --> 00:04:45.650
is it a car with
a computer in it,

00:04:45.650 --> 00:04:48.110
or is it a smartphone
on wheels, right?

00:04:48.110 --> 00:04:49.820
What really is it?

00:04:49.820 --> 00:04:52.040
And this has impact
on everything

00:04:52.040 --> 00:04:55.160
from mechanical design,
all the buttons and knobs

00:04:55.160 --> 00:04:57.020
that you would normally
have, still needed

00:04:57.020 --> 00:04:59.840
if you're now going to control
those virtually or via an app,

00:04:59.840 --> 00:05:01.100
right?

00:05:01.100 --> 00:05:04.280
That affects the electrical
design, electronics design,

00:05:04.280 --> 00:05:05.990
connectivity design.

00:05:05.990 --> 00:05:09.680
Are you going to use wired
networking, Bluetooth, Wi-Fi,

00:05:09.680 --> 00:05:11.090
ZigBee cellular?

00:05:11.090 --> 00:05:12.480
Which type of cellular?

00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:14.030
Where in which regions?

00:05:14.030 --> 00:05:16.490
Low power wide area networking,
all these considerations

00:05:16.490 --> 00:05:20.360
that come just into the physical
product that are different

00:05:20.360 --> 00:05:23.960
then if it's not a part
of a connected solution.

00:05:23.960 --> 00:05:27.860
The Cloud platform, as you're
picking a cloud partner,

00:05:27.860 --> 00:05:31.970
does that partner bring the
global scale security, tools,

00:05:31.970 --> 00:05:34.210
capabilities that
you need to have

00:05:34.210 --> 00:05:38.642
to be the platform upon which a
lot of this solution is built?

00:05:38.642 --> 00:05:40.100
And the user
application, so that's

00:05:40.100 --> 00:05:41.850
kind of an interesting
one, because if you

00:05:41.850 --> 00:05:44.720
look at two and four,
user applications

00:05:44.720 --> 00:05:47.750
and the connected product,
these are two areas

00:05:47.750 --> 00:05:51.170
where you're building the part
that will interact directly

00:05:51.170 --> 00:05:52.400
with the customer.

00:05:52.400 --> 00:05:55.580
And one is physical
and one is digital.

00:05:55.580 --> 00:05:57.110
And you have this
kind of interplay

00:05:57.110 --> 00:05:59.120
between the physical
and digital world,

00:05:59.120 --> 00:06:02.540
and then IoT, as you
know, a lot of the value

00:06:02.540 --> 00:06:05.060
starts to shift from the
traditional physical product

00:06:05.060 --> 00:06:06.680
into the digital world.

00:06:06.680 --> 00:06:09.110
But this brings up some
opportunities, of course,

00:06:09.110 --> 00:06:10.627
but also some
challenges, and maybe

00:06:10.627 --> 00:06:11.710
even some culture changes.

00:06:11.710 --> 00:06:14.390
I think, Bryan, you'll talk
a little bit about this.

00:06:14.390 --> 00:06:16.373
Because you develop
software a lot differently

00:06:16.373 --> 00:06:18.290
than you develop hardware,
but they have to be

00:06:18.290 --> 00:06:19.730
in sync with each other, right?

00:06:19.730 --> 00:06:21.740
So sometimes there's
even a cultural change

00:06:21.740 --> 00:06:25.490
in a company that has
to take place here.

00:06:25.490 --> 00:06:29.570
Then the next step,
hopefully when

00:06:29.570 --> 00:06:31.652
you're embarking on an IoT--

00:06:31.652 --> 00:06:33.110
on the IoT journey,
you're not just

00:06:33.110 --> 00:06:34.610
building a connected product.

00:06:34.610 --> 00:06:37.760
But you are really running
a connected business

00:06:37.760 --> 00:06:39.830
for the benefit
of your customers.

00:06:39.830 --> 00:06:42.350
And what this often
means is how do you

00:06:42.350 --> 00:06:46.340
integrate data from these
services into business systems

00:06:46.340 --> 00:06:48.560
that you use to run
your business every day?

00:06:48.560 --> 00:06:52.040
If that's a CRM system, if
it's a marketing automation

00:06:52.040 --> 00:06:57.220
platform, if it's an ERP system,
if it's a ticketing system,

00:06:57.220 --> 00:06:59.780
[INAUDIBLE],, that's
something to really

00:06:59.780 --> 00:07:02.750
think about, the capabilities
of those platforms

00:07:02.750 --> 00:07:07.310
and the limitations also and
how that ties into the equation.

00:07:07.310 --> 00:07:12.380
Next, who's heard IoT's
all about the data, right?

00:07:12.380 --> 00:07:13.253
Show of hands.

00:07:13.253 --> 00:07:14.420
The hands are a little weak.

00:07:14.420 --> 00:07:15.337
Everyone's heard that.

00:07:15.337 --> 00:07:16.730
Or data is the new oil.

00:07:16.730 --> 00:07:19.060
We've got to change this to
be a little more renewable.

00:07:19.060 --> 00:07:20.728
I think we should
start saying in IoT,

00:07:20.728 --> 00:07:21.770
data is the new sunshine.

00:07:24.470 --> 00:07:25.850
Is it too soft?

00:07:25.850 --> 00:07:26.990
Data is the new sunshine?

00:07:26.990 --> 00:07:28.215
I think-- I think we could--

00:07:28.215 --> 00:07:29.510
let's tag it right now.

00:07:29.510 --> 00:07:31.400
That's the first
time it was said.

00:07:31.400 --> 00:07:36.050
But the point is that IoT
is more than just connecting

00:07:36.050 --> 00:07:37.720
devices and connecting people.

00:07:37.720 --> 00:07:41.480
There is a lot that can be done
and needs to be thought out

00:07:41.480 --> 00:07:42.390
with the data.

00:07:42.390 --> 00:07:44.030
What types of new
insights can you

00:07:44.030 --> 00:07:46.070
derive that you could
never derive before?

00:07:46.070 --> 00:07:47.900
What understanding
of a customer can you

00:07:47.900 --> 00:07:50.090
have that you were never
able to have before?

00:07:50.090 --> 00:07:54.960
And this blending of IoT
with smart analytics,

00:07:54.960 --> 00:07:56.953
artificial intelligence,
and machine learning

00:07:56.953 --> 00:07:58.620
is absolutely a trend
that we're seeing,

00:07:58.620 --> 00:08:02.910
and we the industry is
evolving towards as well.

00:08:02.910 --> 00:08:09.490
Finally-- and this is
probably the most overlooked--

00:08:09.490 --> 00:08:15.947
when a company goes after
the opportunity of the IoT,

00:08:15.947 --> 00:08:18.280
the companies that also do a
really good job of thinking

00:08:18.280 --> 00:08:21.790
about the obligations that
they now are taking on

00:08:21.790 --> 00:08:25.780
by becoming a 24 by 7 service
provider is really important.

00:08:25.780 --> 00:08:28.870
Because in balancing the
opportunity and obligation,

00:08:28.870 --> 00:08:30.563
when you're a 24 by
7 service provider,

00:08:30.563 --> 00:08:32.230
you have a different
support expectation

00:08:32.230 --> 00:08:33.370
from your customers.

00:08:33.370 --> 00:08:34.780
They expect that 24 by 7.

00:08:34.780 --> 00:08:39.073
And if you bake that into the
business case and the solution,

00:08:39.073 --> 00:08:40.490
you have the whole
thing together.

00:08:40.490 --> 00:08:42.850
So it starts and ends
with the customer.

00:08:42.850 --> 00:08:44.900
Starts and ends
with the customer.

00:08:44.900 --> 00:08:49.040
So two more quick slides, and
then I'm going to pass it on.

00:08:49.040 --> 00:08:51.770
If you're with-- if you spend
much time with me or my team,

00:08:51.770 --> 00:08:54.170
you're going to hear
that you cannot--

00:08:54.170 --> 00:08:56.690
no company can do IoT alone.

00:08:56.690 --> 00:08:59.660
And what we kind of jokingly
say, but seriously say too

00:08:59.660 --> 00:09:03.740
is there's always three legs
to the stool to implementing

00:09:03.740 --> 00:09:04.950
an IoT solution.

00:09:04.950 --> 00:09:07.790
There's a platform, an
enterprise class platform,

00:09:07.790 --> 00:09:09.680
like Google Cloud
Platform is the foundation

00:09:09.680 --> 00:09:12.740
upon which a lot is
built. Then there's

00:09:12.740 --> 00:09:16.850
an ecosystem of partners,
a best in class partners

00:09:16.850 --> 00:09:19.910
that really provide the
hardware-software connectivity,

00:09:19.910 --> 00:09:22.970
and those pieces that
really either fill in gaps,

00:09:22.970 --> 00:09:25.810
or accelerate innovation
from customers.

00:09:25.810 --> 00:09:28.040
Now, Aeris is a great
example of a partner

00:09:28.040 --> 00:09:31.110
that provides us full stack
IoT solution with Google Cloud.

00:09:31.110 --> 00:09:33.170
You'll hear a lot
more about that.

00:09:33.170 --> 00:09:35.390
And then you always
need pro services.

00:09:35.390 --> 00:09:37.408
So platform, partners,
and pro services.

00:09:37.408 --> 00:09:39.200
Because no solution is
complete, you always

00:09:39.200 --> 00:09:42.260
need experts, either domain
experts or technology experts,

00:09:42.260 --> 00:09:44.340
to help bring it all together.

00:09:44.340 --> 00:09:47.120
So we as Google Cloud
with respect to IoT,

00:09:47.120 --> 00:09:49.753
we never engage with customers
without bringing in partners.

00:09:49.753 --> 00:09:51.170
We're always
bringing in partners.

00:09:51.170 --> 00:09:52.670
And there's always--
the three legs

00:09:52.670 --> 00:09:56.660
of the stool are always there
to stand up the solution.

00:09:56.660 --> 00:10:01.360
And lastly, I'll spend the
least amount of time on this.

00:10:01.360 --> 00:10:03.840
But if you look at from the
platform perspective, what

00:10:03.840 --> 00:10:08.400
Google Cloud brings is a
set of managed services that

00:10:08.400 --> 00:10:12.150
allow companies to connect
millions of devices and just

00:10:12.150 --> 00:10:14.760
that data, process,
clean, store that data,

00:10:14.760 --> 00:10:18.060
and then develop those
insights and business

00:10:18.060 --> 00:10:19.710
outcomes that you want.

00:10:19.710 --> 00:10:22.740
And this is a platform
that with the other two

00:10:22.740 --> 00:10:25.380
legs of the stool, brings
the whole solution together

00:10:25.380 --> 00:10:27.130
for customers.

00:10:27.130 --> 00:10:30.030
So with that, I'd
love to hand it off

00:10:30.030 --> 00:10:31.800
to Bryan, who's
going to tell you

00:10:31.800 --> 00:10:33.967
a little bit about Mitsubishi.

00:10:33.967 --> 00:10:34.800
BRYAN ARNETT: Great.

00:10:34.800 --> 00:10:35.300
Thank you.

00:10:38.080 --> 00:10:39.400
Good morning, everyone.

00:10:39.400 --> 00:10:40.860
My name is Bryan Arnett.

00:10:40.860 --> 00:10:44.520
I am a-- not an IoT guy.

00:10:44.520 --> 00:10:45.770
MARIO FINOCCHIARO: [INAUDIBLE]

00:10:45.770 --> 00:10:47.670
BRYAN ARNETT: Oh,
I need this thing.

00:10:47.670 --> 00:10:49.110
As you can see.

00:10:49.110 --> 00:10:51.210
I am actually-- I'm
a product planner.

00:10:51.210 --> 00:10:53.850
I've been a product planner
for the OEM industry

00:10:53.850 --> 00:10:56.710
for over 25 years.

00:10:56.710 --> 00:10:59.250
I've worked with--

00:10:59.250 --> 00:11:01.350
I currently work with
Mitsubishi Motors,

00:11:01.350 --> 00:11:04.770
and was recently tasked
about two years ago

00:11:04.770 --> 00:11:07.950
to investigate as part of the
product planning operation

00:11:07.950 --> 00:11:12.750
at Mitsubishi Motors to get
into this telematics game.

00:11:12.750 --> 00:11:14.130
We saw a lot of--

00:11:14.130 --> 00:11:16.590
as you guys know, there's
the General Motors,

00:11:16.590 --> 00:11:19.710
and a lot of the OEMs
out there are all

00:11:19.710 --> 00:11:22.770
talking about connected
cars and what connected cars

00:11:22.770 --> 00:11:24.750
means to the OEM industry.

00:11:24.750 --> 00:11:27.460
And we needed to
figure this out.

00:11:27.460 --> 00:11:28.990
We need to get
part of this game.

00:11:28.990 --> 00:11:34.260
So we started to investigate
the telematics industry.

00:11:34.260 --> 00:11:38.080
And we started talking with
many different suppliers,

00:11:38.080 --> 00:11:40.860
including Aeris.

00:11:40.860 --> 00:11:45.690
What we quickly learned is
that the biggest lesson of all

00:11:45.690 --> 00:11:49.350
is we come from two
completely different worlds.

00:11:49.350 --> 00:11:54.150
In the automotive industry,
we have been building cars

00:11:54.150 --> 00:12:00.100
for 100 years using waterfall
approach to engineering.

00:12:00.100 --> 00:12:04.390
Our engineers are basically
ingrained in their methodology

00:12:04.390 --> 00:12:07.090
and go through a series
of waterfall check-ins.

00:12:07.090 --> 00:12:08.770
And so suddenly, when
we start bringing

00:12:08.770 --> 00:12:13.240
in IoT into the onboard
environment, into the vehicle,

00:12:13.240 --> 00:12:17.530
we start to realize that
we need to blend these two

00:12:17.530 --> 00:12:18.220
worlds together.

00:12:18.220 --> 00:12:20.530
We need to have some
type of a hybrid approach

00:12:20.530 --> 00:12:23.290
between waterfall development
and agile development.

00:12:23.290 --> 00:12:27.010
And so what we're finding is
that there's two cultures.

00:12:27.010 --> 00:12:28.720
And we as an automotive
manufacturer,

00:12:28.720 --> 00:12:32.080
we need to learn how
to be more agile.

00:12:32.080 --> 00:12:34.510
And you'll hear that from
probably a lot of OEMs

00:12:34.510 --> 00:12:36.910
out there from the
automotive side.

00:12:36.910 --> 00:12:39.040
You'll read how
automotive manufacturers

00:12:39.040 --> 00:12:43.400
need to think faster and need to
move faster to be competitive.

00:12:43.400 --> 00:12:47.380
And so by partnering with Aeris
and working on the IoT space,

00:12:47.380 --> 00:12:51.190
we actually learned how
to improve our culture

00:12:51.190 --> 00:12:53.770
from an engineering
perspective to implement more

00:12:53.770 --> 00:12:55.540
an agile approach
to our development,

00:12:55.540 --> 00:12:58.630
and start rethinking basically
how we develop vehicles

00:12:58.630 --> 00:13:01.390
from a more agile point of
view, not just a waterfall

00:13:01.390 --> 00:13:03.610
point of view.

00:13:03.610 --> 00:13:06.120
So like any good
connected car program,

00:13:06.120 --> 00:13:08.300
we need to start with a vision.

00:13:08.300 --> 00:13:10.240
So what is it that we want?

00:13:10.240 --> 00:13:12.880
So essentially, we
encapsulated our vision

00:13:12.880 --> 00:13:16.000
as the car will be integrated
into the IoT network

00:13:16.000 --> 00:13:19.120
by advanced connected car
technology of Mitsubishi

00:13:19.120 --> 00:13:20.080
Motors.

00:13:20.080 --> 00:13:22.670
In the future, our
connection to each other

00:13:22.670 --> 00:13:25.120
and the world around
us will only grow.

00:13:25.120 --> 00:13:27.250
We will be part of the
Internet of Things,

00:13:27.250 --> 00:13:29.800
connecting you more
directly to your world.

00:13:29.800 --> 00:13:32.530
Mitsubishi connect will provide
innovative connected services

00:13:32.530 --> 00:13:34.000
by applying the
information network

00:13:34.000 --> 00:13:37.150
to provide a more personalized
and rich driving experience.

00:13:37.150 --> 00:13:38.860
And what is really
important here

00:13:38.860 --> 00:13:40.390
is the statement
where it says we

00:13:40.390 --> 00:13:42.015
will be part of the
Internet of Things,

00:13:42.015 --> 00:13:44.690
connecting you more
directly to your world.

00:13:44.690 --> 00:13:47.890
So a lot of times
manufacturers think of things

00:13:47.890 --> 00:13:49.150
as a hardware perspective.

00:13:49.150 --> 00:13:52.180
We connect a cell
phone to a car,

00:13:52.180 --> 00:13:54.970
and we do a remote engine
start, and we're done.

00:13:54.970 --> 00:13:56.470
What we need to
start thinking about

00:13:56.470 --> 00:14:00.590
is connecting the customer to
the car, not just the devices.

00:14:00.590 --> 00:14:03.400
And so this is also something
that automotive OEMs

00:14:03.400 --> 00:14:05.080
are starting to think about.

00:14:05.080 --> 00:14:07.840
How do we connect our
customers into our world,

00:14:07.840 --> 00:14:09.310
not just bring our device--

00:14:09.310 --> 00:14:11.890
bring their devices
into our car.

00:14:11.890 --> 00:14:15.580
So that's something that is
very important as we also

00:14:15.580 --> 00:14:19.510
look at what is our
strategy going forward.

00:14:19.510 --> 00:14:21.970
The US traditionally has
been the largest market

00:14:21.970 --> 00:14:25.090
for telematics services
in the United States.

00:14:25.090 --> 00:14:27.370
Almost all OEM major
brands now have

00:14:27.370 --> 00:14:33.310
some sort of aggressive fitment
penetration in connected cars.

00:14:33.310 --> 00:14:36.640
And as you know,
traditionally, most

00:14:36.640 --> 00:14:43.120
of the automotive profitability,
over a majority of it,

00:14:43.120 --> 00:14:46.510
is traditionally gained by
fixed operation expenses, sale

00:14:46.510 --> 00:14:49.900
of oil, sale of parts,
maintenance, services,

00:14:49.900 --> 00:14:51.520
things like that.

00:14:51.520 --> 00:14:54.040
With the growth of
electric vehicles

00:14:54.040 --> 00:14:57.140
rapidly being realized
in the marketplace,

00:14:57.140 --> 00:15:00.040
some of these traditional
fixed operational services

00:15:00.040 --> 00:15:01.780
and some of these
traditional revenues

00:15:01.780 --> 00:15:03.590
are going to disappear.

00:15:03.590 --> 00:15:05.770
So we need to start
thinking about new business

00:15:05.770 --> 00:15:09.760
models and new ways to
generate new future service

00:15:09.760 --> 00:15:12.220
revenues for things in
the automotive sector

00:15:12.220 --> 00:15:14.470
and becoming more
connected to our customers

00:15:14.470 --> 00:15:18.347
and delivering more on-demand
related services through IoT.

00:15:18.347 --> 00:15:19.930
You can see from the
chart here, which

00:15:19.930 --> 00:15:23.410
is provided by SPD
Automotive, by 2025,

00:15:23.410 --> 00:15:26.530
over 90% of all vehicles
shipped to the United States

00:15:26.530 --> 00:15:30.040
will have an embedded
connected, and also

00:15:30.040 --> 00:15:32.900
a smartphone-tethered
device in the vehicle.

00:15:32.900 --> 00:15:35.780
So within a very
short period of time,

00:15:35.780 --> 00:15:38.260
within the next
five years or so,

00:15:38.260 --> 00:15:42.010
virtually every vehicle
shipped in the United States

00:15:42.010 --> 00:15:44.680
will have some sort
of connected device.

00:15:44.680 --> 00:15:48.850
So you're looking at a SAAR of
about 17 million new cars sold

00:15:48.850 --> 00:15:50.090
every year.

00:15:50.090 --> 00:15:53.680
So by 2025, you're going to
see a rapid expansion of IoT

00:15:53.680 --> 00:15:56.710
and services being offered
in the automotive space.

00:15:56.710 --> 00:16:00.340
So we have only five
years to really learn

00:16:00.340 --> 00:16:02.610
how to get this right.

00:16:02.610 --> 00:16:04.200
So from Mitsubishi's
perspective,

00:16:04.200 --> 00:16:08.220
we looked at it from kind
of a three-stage approach.

00:16:08.220 --> 00:16:10.470
I guess this is kind
of like your stool.

00:16:10.470 --> 00:16:13.360
First, we looked at it
from phase one perspective.

00:16:13.360 --> 00:16:15.750
So we introduced something
called a Smartphone Link

00:16:15.750 --> 00:16:17.370
Display Audio.

00:16:17.370 --> 00:16:20.190
Most people know
this as Android Auto.

00:16:20.190 --> 00:16:23.850
We introduced the Android Auto
capability into our vehicles,

00:16:23.850 --> 00:16:27.060
and we have a tethered
smartphone system

00:16:27.060 --> 00:16:30.690
in our vehicle where we can do
custom OEM applications that

00:16:30.690 --> 00:16:35.310
are approved by Google and can
be downloaded from the Google

00:16:35.310 --> 00:16:38.190
App Store and put on your
cell phone, and so forth.

00:16:38.190 --> 00:16:40.630
Then beginning in
2018, just last year,

00:16:40.630 --> 00:16:42.990
we introduced Mitsubishi
Connect, which is basically

00:16:42.990 --> 00:16:44.880
a TCU, or a Telematics
Control Unit,

00:16:44.880 --> 00:16:48.570
or an embedded device that is
installed into the vehicle.

00:16:48.570 --> 00:16:50.580
And then next, for
our phase three,

00:16:50.580 --> 00:16:52.110
we get into more
of the analytics.

00:16:52.110 --> 00:16:54.647
And this is where we are
going to start learning more

00:16:54.647 --> 00:16:56.730
about our customers, how
to service our customers,

00:16:56.730 --> 00:16:59.490
and how to grow our
business, and then

00:16:59.490 --> 00:17:02.520
the service retention of our
customers through the analytics

00:17:02.520 --> 00:17:04.859
that we're going to gain
through both the data that

00:17:04.859 --> 00:17:06.510
comes from the TCU,
and the data that

00:17:06.510 --> 00:17:11.270
comes from the tethered device.

00:17:11.270 --> 00:17:14.329
So today, Mitsubishi Connect
system is in the market.

00:17:14.329 --> 00:17:16.200
It launched about a year ago.

00:17:16.200 --> 00:17:20.730
We offer a two-year we call
Safeguard and Remote Service

00:17:20.730 --> 00:17:24.150
bundle with the purchase
of any Mitsubishi vehicle

00:17:24.150 --> 00:17:25.720
that is equipped with this.

00:17:25.720 --> 00:17:28.560
So Safeguard Services
include things

00:17:28.560 --> 00:17:32.820
like automatic collision
notification, SOS emergency

00:17:32.820 --> 00:17:36.840
assistance, roadside assistance,
stolen vehicle tracking.

00:17:36.840 --> 00:17:40.080
Remote services include things
like remote climate control,

00:17:40.080 --> 00:17:43.180
remote door lock, car
finder, parental settings,

00:17:43.180 --> 00:17:46.910
things like that, all of
which offer connectivity

00:17:46.910 --> 00:17:47.910
through your smartphone.

00:17:47.910 --> 00:17:51.420
And you can see the application
there in the center.

00:17:51.420 --> 00:17:54.240
We also offer Google
Home capability, where

00:17:54.240 --> 00:17:57.150
you can use Google,
talk to your car

00:17:57.150 --> 00:17:59.970
through the Google Home
device to remote start.

00:17:59.970 --> 00:18:01.620
And so now we're
starting to establish

00:18:01.620 --> 00:18:04.020
a relationship between
the customer and the car

00:18:04.020 --> 00:18:06.872
more directly when
you're not in the car.

00:18:06.872 --> 00:18:08.080
And so that's very important.

00:18:08.080 --> 00:18:12.150
Typically, OEMs only were
able to make a relationship

00:18:12.150 --> 00:18:14.580
or establish some kind of
connection with the customer

00:18:14.580 --> 00:18:17.880
when they were driving and
when they were in the car.

00:18:17.880 --> 00:18:19.620
Now we're able to
start establishing

00:18:19.620 --> 00:18:21.360
some type of a
relationship and starting

00:18:21.360 --> 00:18:23.787
to be able to provide
some branding exercise

00:18:23.787 --> 00:18:25.870
to those customers when
they're away from the car.

00:18:25.870 --> 00:18:27.780
And so when they have
their smartphone,

00:18:27.780 --> 00:18:31.410
or they're at home in the
morning getting ready to go,

00:18:31.410 --> 00:18:33.720
just simply ask Google
to start their car,

00:18:33.720 --> 00:18:37.557
precondition the
climate control.

00:18:37.557 --> 00:18:39.140
So some of the key
business challenges

00:18:39.140 --> 00:18:41.420
that we faced that
we were looking at,

00:18:41.420 --> 00:18:46.130
Mitsubishi generally is
a much smaller company

00:18:46.130 --> 00:18:48.650
than the Toyotas or the
Volkswagens of the world.

00:18:48.650 --> 00:18:52.940
So economies of scale were very
important and necessary for us

00:18:52.940 --> 00:18:54.830
to make a valid business case.

00:18:54.830 --> 00:18:57.428
We talked a little earlier
about having the business case.

00:18:57.428 --> 00:18:58.970
So of course, we
had a business case.

00:18:58.970 --> 00:19:01.430
And we knew right away that
in order to make this work,

00:19:01.430 --> 00:19:03.403
we needed to do it
on a global scale.

00:19:03.403 --> 00:19:05.820
We did not necessarily have
the volumes only in the United

00:19:05.820 --> 00:19:07.362
States, or we didn't
have the volumes

00:19:07.362 --> 00:19:08.810
only in the European market.

00:19:08.810 --> 00:19:12.470
So we needed to look at this
from a big global perspective.

00:19:12.470 --> 00:19:14.720
And so we started looking
at what kind of partnerships

00:19:14.720 --> 00:19:17.100
we could have in
that perspective.

00:19:17.100 --> 00:19:19.650
And quite frankly,
there weren't many,

00:19:19.650 --> 00:19:21.740
with the exception
of maybe Aeris.

00:19:21.740 --> 00:19:25.790
So we needed somebody to deliver
automotive grade IoT services

00:19:25.790 --> 00:19:28.370
on a global scale.

00:19:28.370 --> 00:19:32.150
We needed a vendor to help us
to provide technical leadership

00:19:32.150 --> 00:19:34.880
and manage the complexities
that we were quickly

00:19:34.880 --> 00:19:37.310
realizing, as an
automotive manufacturer,

00:19:37.310 --> 00:19:39.560
we weren't experts in IoT.

00:19:39.560 --> 00:19:41.000
We were car guys.

00:19:41.000 --> 00:19:45.260
We needed somebody who
could help us become experts

00:19:45.260 --> 00:19:48.140
and guide us in the IoT place.

00:19:48.140 --> 00:19:50.210
And lastly, we
needed somebody that

00:19:50.210 --> 00:19:53.870
could be flexible and adapt
to the automotive processes

00:19:53.870 --> 00:19:57.460
and change requests
that we constantly make.

00:19:57.460 --> 00:19:59.150
We're a waterfall
development company.

00:19:59.150 --> 00:20:00.710
We're learning as we go.

00:20:00.710 --> 00:20:04.880
We needed somebody that was
flexible and easy to work with,

00:20:04.880 --> 00:20:09.170
and understood that we're
learning along with them.

00:20:09.170 --> 00:20:12.800
So one of the partners here
that will speak next is Aeris.

00:20:12.800 --> 00:20:15.680
They have delivered on
a lot of this for us

00:20:15.680 --> 00:20:19.460
and have been able to be
really instrumental in helping

00:20:19.460 --> 00:20:21.920
us manage the complexity,
and helping me

00:20:21.920 --> 00:20:28.500
and our team at Mitsubishi
worldwide learn the IoT space.

00:20:28.500 --> 00:20:30.980
The next thing we
needed was Mitsubishi

00:20:30.980 --> 00:20:32.840
needed an end-to-end
modular solution that

00:20:32.840 --> 00:20:38.180
could adapt globally and grow
at the speed of the feature

00:20:38.180 --> 00:20:38.910
innovation.

00:20:38.910 --> 00:20:43.400
So we needed to look at the
stack of technology that

00:20:43.400 --> 00:20:44.540
was available.

00:20:44.540 --> 00:20:47.640
And here's basically a
picture of Aeris full stack

00:20:47.640 --> 00:20:48.450
of services.

00:20:48.450 --> 00:20:51.230
So the edge client, the
connectivity manager,

00:20:51.230 --> 00:20:55.160
the IoT transition
services, the core services,

00:20:55.160 --> 00:20:58.238
all the TSP services, including
the call center operations.

00:20:58.238 --> 00:20:59.780
You can see the
complexity gets quite

00:20:59.780 --> 00:21:02.610
overwhelming from an
automotive manufacturer.

00:21:02.610 --> 00:21:04.910
So we needed somebody
to handle all of this,

00:21:04.910 --> 00:21:06.410
and then we also
needed somebody who

00:21:06.410 --> 00:21:08.985
would be able to be flexible
with this stack in case

00:21:08.985 --> 00:21:10.610
something were to
change in the future,

00:21:10.610 --> 00:21:12.590
or be able to adjust this stack.

00:21:12.590 --> 00:21:15.950
So this is one of
the key requirements

00:21:15.950 --> 00:21:18.747
we had of a supplier.

00:21:18.747 --> 00:21:21.330
And then lastly, as I mentioned,
we needed the ability to work

00:21:21.330 --> 00:21:25.930
with our MNO partner-- we
currently work with AT&amp;T--

00:21:25.930 --> 00:21:27.690
the mobile network
operators to provide

00:21:27.690 --> 00:21:31.050
seamless 4G LTE connectivity,
SIM provisioning,

00:21:31.050 --> 00:21:33.630
turning on the SIM chip,
turning off the SIM chip,

00:21:33.630 --> 00:21:36.930
managing the
subscriber statuses.

00:21:36.930 --> 00:21:38.770
All of that needed to be done.

00:21:38.770 --> 00:21:42.000
The call center
authentication tool

00:21:42.000 --> 00:21:44.410
needed to be developed
from a global perspective,

00:21:44.410 --> 00:21:47.160
not just in the United
States, but also in Europe

00:21:47.160 --> 00:21:50.310
and around the world.

00:21:50.310 --> 00:21:52.130
And then we also needed--

00:21:52.130 --> 00:21:54.650
we needed a kind
of connectivity,

00:21:54.650 --> 00:21:58.490
a connected vehicle ecosystem
that provides significant--

00:21:58.490 --> 00:22:00.950
that provides a significant
threat for hackers.

00:22:00.950 --> 00:22:05.600
Basically, the industry needs
to have a secure vehicle, right?

00:22:05.600 --> 00:22:08.390
As we start getting
into the IoT space,

00:22:08.390 --> 00:22:10.280
and we start bringing
the outside world

00:22:10.280 --> 00:22:15.290
kind of into our vehicle, one of
the biggest concerns of an OEM

00:22:15.290 --> 00:22:18.410
is the security of the vehicle.

00:22:18.410 --> 00:22:21.515
Obviously, we are a very
heavily regulated industry.

00:22:21.515 --> 00:22:23.390
I think there is a
government agency for just

00:22:23.390 --> 00:22:26.320
about everything related to
the connected car with NTSA,

00:22:26.320 --> 00:22:29.060
or the Homeland Security, or
Department of Transportation,

00:22:29.060 --> 00:22:29.840
or whatever.

00:22:29.840 --> 00:22:33.650
We are constantly having
to report to the government

00:22:33.650 --> 00:22:34.280
authorities.

00:22:34.280 --> 00:22:38.150
And so here comes IoT,
and we're, oh, god,

00:22:38.150 --> 00:22:40.772
what government authority do
we have to report to now when

00:22:40.772 --> 00:22:41.480
it comes to this?

00:22:41.480 --> 00:22:44.510
So security is one of
the things we definitely

00:22:44.510 --> 00:22:46.730
wanted to make sure we
had a good handle on.

00:22:46.730 --> 00:22:50.090
So we need to secure the vehicle
while protecting the driver.

00:22:50.090 --> 00:22:52.640
We needed the ability to
update the hardware, our apps,

00:22:52.640 --> 00:22:55.447
and our services without
inconveniencing our customers.

00:22:55.447 --> 00:22:57.530
And we needed to be able
to differentiate services

00:22:57.530 --> 00:23:00.698
based upon the identity
of the primary owner,

00:23:00.698 --> 00:23:02.240
or the secondary
owner, or whether it

00:23:02.240 --> 00:23:04.880
was a fleet customer, or
was it a retail customer.

00:23:04.880 --> 00:23:07.730
We have many different
types of customers when it

00:23:07.730 --> 00:23:09.380
comes to purchasing vehicles.

00:23:09.380 --> 00:23:13.100
And so it's very important
when connecting a car

00:23:13.100 --> 00:23:16.410
to not just connect the car,
but to connect the customer.

00:23:16.410 --> 00:23:18.180
And so in order to
connect a customer,

00:23:18.180 --> 00:23:19.980
we have to know who
that customer is.

00:23:19.980 --> 00:23:22.140
We have to be able to
control and manage that.

00:23:22.140 --> 00:23:25.640
So that's very, very important,
and Aeris has delivered

00:23:25.640 --> 00:23:28.770
on many of those areas.

00:23:28.770 --> 00:23:30.780
So that's it for me.

00:23:30.780 --> 00:23:32.390
I'd like turn it over to Drew.

00:23:32.390 --> 00:23:34.820
And he can talk to you a
bit about the technical side

00:23:34.820 --> 00:23:38.410
of the IoT integration.

00:23:38.410 --> 00:23:39.910
DREW JOHNSON: Thank you, Bryan.

00:23:39.910 --> 00:23:41.500
And thank you.

00:23:41.500 --> 00:23:44.440
We're so proud, and
grateful, actually,

00:23:44.440 --> 00:23:47.370
to have Mitsubishi as
a customer of ours.

00:23:47.370 --> 00:23:48.880
It's fantastic.

00:23:48.880 --> 00:23:53.260
And I'll just say we use
the word automotive grade,

00:23:53.260 --> 00:23:58.570
those words, actually, as a
bit of a proxy for reliability,

00:23:58.570 --> 00:24:01.930
and security, and complexity.

00:24:01.930 --> 00:24:06.340
And we're really happy
that even though this

00:24:06.340 --> 00:24:11.680
is a very mature market
relative to many IoT markets,

00:24:11.680 --> 00:24:17.170
that our platform, we're winning
among quite a few automakers,

00:24:17.170 --> 00:24:19.630
which is actually very
difficult to become

00:24:19.630 --> 00:24:21.260
a supplier of automakers.

00:24:21.260 --> 00:24:24.252
So that automotive grade
really does mean something.

00:24:27.150 --> 00:24:31.290
So if we kind of go through
several of the key challenges

00:24:31.290 --> 00:24:36.240
that Bryan mentioned, one
of them is complexity.

00:24:36.240 --> 00:24:40.710
And actually, complexity is one
of the key challenges overall

00:24:40.710 --> 00:24:42.060
in the Internet of Things.

00:24:42.060 --> 00:24:45.240
It's very difficult,
especially for OEMs who are--

00:24:45.240 --> 00:24:48.390
as Bryan mentioned,
generally, their business

00:24:48.390 --> 00:24:52.840
has been about making hardware,
potentially for 100 years,

00:24:52.840 --> 00:24:55.080
like in the automotive space.

00:24:55.080 --> 00:25:00.030
And trying to get to
services and software

00:25:00.030 --> 00:25:01.480
can be really challenging.

00:25:01.480 --> 00:25:04.020
And there's a lot of
complexity in that.

00:25:04.020 --> 00:25:07.890
This picture over
here on the right

00:25:07.890 --> 00:25:10.770
is just one view of
some of that complexity.

00:25:10.770 --> 00:25:13.480
There there's other views that--

00:25:13.480 --> 00:25:17.560
different views of how that
complexity takes shape.

00:25:17.560 --> 00:25:19.650
And one of the things
that Mitsubishi

00:25:19.650 --> 00:25:24.570
did that was pretty smart to
help manage that complexity was

00:25:24.570 --> 00:25:26.130
they created what
I kind of think

00:25:26.130 --> 00:25:29.040
of as this pyramid
of responsibility.

00:25:29.040 --> 00:25:33.240
So they really only
had two main suppliers.

00:25:33.240 --> 00:25:37.900
They had Aeris, and they had
their hardware TCU provider.

00:25:37.900 --> 00:25:42.180
And then they counted
on those two providers

00:25:42.180 --> 00:25:44.260
to deliver everything else.

00:25:44.260 --> 00:25:47.850
So if you kind of go
back to Mario's picture,

00:25:47.850 --> 00:25:50.610
pretty much everything
else was delivered

00:25:50.610 --> 00:25:52.260
by those two providers.

00:25:52.260 --> 00:25:54.840
So Aeris, we
delivered the platform

00:25:54.840 --> 00:25:56.640
with all of those
remote operations

00:25:56.640 --> 00:26:01.110
and the analytics that's
there, the UIs, the mobile UI

00:26:01.110 --> 00:26:06.540
and the smart speaker UIs,
the call center, the cloud,

00:26:06.540 --> 00:26:07.870
actually, and the connectivity.

00:26:07.870 --> 00:26:10.860
We managed all the connectivity,
as Bryan mentioned.

00:26:10.860 --> 00:26:14.160
And then along with
the TCU provider,

00:26:14.160 --> 00:26:15.642
we actually shared
responsibility

00:26:15.642 --> 00:26:16.350
for the firmware.

00:26:16.350 --> 00:26:18.642
And I'll talk a little bit
more about that in a minute.

00:26:22.830 --> 00:26:27.450
OK, and then here
on the right, this

00:26:27.450 --> 00:26:31.290
is a picture of our stacks.

00:26:31.290 --> 00:26:35.430
Very simplified
picture of our stack.

00:26:35.430 --> 00:26:38.490
And if we go to,
again, one of the key

00:26:38.490 --> 00:26:42.450
challenges that Bryan
mentioned of how

00:26:42.450 --> 00:26:47.490
do you actually deliver rapidly,
both for the initial launch,

00:26:47.490 --> 00:26:52.430
and then even being able to add
additional services ongoing.

00:26:52.430 --> 00:26:56.130
If you think about a car, it's
in the market for 10 years.

00:26:56.130 --> 00:26:59.970
So you really don't want to
have those features frozen

00:26:59.970 --> 00:27:01.472
for 10 years.

00:27:01.472 --> 00:27:03.180
And you have to have
an architecture then

00:27:03.180 --> 00:27:06.600
that can support
that agility, not

00:27:06.600 --> 00:27:09.300
just in the delivery
of the initial vehicle,

00:27:09.300 --> 00:27:13.410
but actually ongoing over
that 10-year lifecycle.

00:27:13.410 --> 00:27:16.800
So this architecture is key.

00:27:16.800 --> 00:27:20.070
I'll just walk you
through really quickly.

00:27:20.070 --> 00:27:23.220
Each one of these
kind of major boxes

00:27:23.220 --> 00:27:28.332
here has actually something like
10 to 20 microservices in it.

00:27:28.332 --> 00:27:30.040
So I'm not going to
go through all those,

00:27:30.040 --> 00:27:32.265
but I will walk you
through generally

00:27:32.265 --> 00:27:33.390
how the architecture looks.

00:27:33.390 --> 00:27:36.600
So starting down
here, the edge client,

00:27:36.600 --> 00:27:40.810
that's actually software
running in the vehicle.

00:27:40.810 --> 00:27:46.140
So that's actually where we
collaborated with the TCU maker

00:27:46.140 --> 00:27:49.230
to allow us to put that
edge client in the vehicle.

00:27:49.230 --> 00:27:54.060
And that is-- if you look
at previous generations

00:27:54.060 --> 00:27:57.390
of connected cars, that
is relatively unique.

00:27:57.390 --> 00:27:59.430
A lot of the previous
generation connected cars

00:27:59.430 --> 00:28:03.360
did not have an edge
client that was really

00:28:03.360 --> 00:28:06.660
an extension of
the Cloud platform.

00:28:06.660 --> 00:28:09.840
They actually just aligned
on APIs or protocols.

00:28:09.840 --> 00:28:15.570
And that actually is one of
the areas that causes problems

00:28:15.570 --> 00:28:22.110
with being able to develop
rapidly and actually

00:28:22.110 --> 00:28:27.360
extend the services and
platform eventually later on.

00:28:27.360 --> 00:28:28.800
So we have the edge client.

00:28:28.800 --> 00:28:30.490
We have our
connectivity platform.

00:28:30.490 --> 00:28:31.948
So our connectivity
platform, we'll

00:28:31.948 --> 00:28:34.320
talk about that
in the next slide.

00:28:34.320 --> 00:28:37.500
The device platform
is actually, again,

00:28:37.500 --> 00:28:43.800
really key for being able
to abstract the vehicle

00:28:43.800 --> 00:28:46.270
from the rest of the services.

00:28:46.270 --> 00:28:50.370
So the edge client, the edge
client includes capabilities,

00:28:50.370 --> 00:28:55.200
like connection management,
authentication, authorization,

00:28:55.200 --> 00:29:02.940
OTA, all of the local
interfaces to actually perform

00:29:02.940 --> 00:29:04.350
remote operations.

00:29:04.350 --> 00:29:06.173
All of those
capabilities are here,

00:29:06.173 --> 00:29:07.590
but it communicates
back and forth

00:29:07.590 --> 00:29:11.940
to the device platform
in an abstraction that

00:29:11.940 --> 00:29:15.840
allows us to just
make changes here

00:29:15.840 --> 00:29:18.600
without having to make changes
in the full device platform,

00:29:18.600 --> 00:29:20.820
or even in the service platform.

00:29:20.820 --> 00:29:23.910
So very key for this
is actually getting

00:29:23.910 --> 00:29:26.982
that abstraction correct.

00:29:26.982 --> 00:29:28.690
And we've seen a lot
of platforms, again,

00:29:28.690 --> 00:29:32.040
where there's tight coupling,
especially between here

00:29:32.040 --> 00:29:35.400
and here, or maybe even
between here and there,

00:29:35.400 --> 00:29:39.900
between the service
platform and the edge

00:29:39.900 --> 00:29:47.618
client that actually constrains
those eventual developments.

00:29:50.830 --> 00:29:55.810
And just a quick note, if
you think about the IoT core,

00:29:55.810 --> 00:29:59.830
the IoT core actually is one
of the microservices here

00:29:59.830 --> 00:30:02.980
in the device platform that's
part of the communications

00:30:02.980 --> 00:30:04.216
infrastructure.

00:30:10.060 --> 00:30:13.390
All right, and then from a
connectivity perspective,

00:30:13.390 --> 00:30:16.120
Aeris is actually-- we
provide connectivity

00:30:16.120 --> 00:30:20.200
for the Internet of Things
in many, many horizontals.

00:30:20.200 --> 00:30:25.240
So we have customers in--
or many, many verticals.

00:30:25.240 --> 00:30:29.530
We have customers probably in
every single Internet of Things

00:30:29.530 --> 00:30:33.290
vertical in the market.

00:30:33.290 --> 00:30:36.970
And what we've seen with
many years of experience,

00:30:36.970 --> 00:30:41.050
is generally, particularly
large programs,

00:30:41.050 --> 00:30:46.750
they fall apart if the
customer really does not

00:30:46.750 --> 00:30:50.140
pay attention to connectivity.

00:30:50.140 --> 00:30:52.390
If you really go into
the market and you

00:30:52.390 --> 00:30:57.790
think cellular connectivity is
more or less similar to Wi-Fi

00:30:57.790 --> 00:31:01.300
or wired and you
ignore the complexities

00:31:01.300 --> 00:31:03.580
of cellular
connectivity, then you'll

00:31:03.580 --> 00:31:05.352
run into problems at scale.

00:31:05.352 --> 00:31:06.310
There will be problems.

00:31:06.310 --> 00:31:10.660
If you really just
think about it,

00:31:10.660 --> 00:31:14.920
if you have problems maybe with
making calls or connections

00:31:14.920 --> 00:31:17.030
in a particular
room of your house,

00:31:17.030 --> 00:31:20.800
you can always move to another
room or reposition yourself,

00:31:20.800 --> 00:31:22.260
but a car can't do that.

00:31:22.260 --> 00:31:25.300
A car sitting in your
garage can't do that.

00:31:25.300 --> 00:31:28.480
So you need to have a
connectivity management

00:31:28.480 --> 00:31:32.290
platform that's really
optimized for handling

00:31:32.290 --> 00:31:37.630
the Internet of Things, which
is very different than what

00:31:37.630 --> 00:31:41.610
you need for consumers
and consumer handsets.

00:31:41.610 --> 00:31:45.330
And then there's other
aspects of cellular that

00:31:45.330 --> 00:31:49.790
come into play, like security.

00:31:49.790 --> 00:31:53.280
And we'll talk a little bit
about security in a minute.

00:31:53.280 --> 00:31:58.680
Being able to
optimize the response

00:31:58.680 --> 00:32:01.570
times across the
cellular connectivity,

00:32:01.570 --> 00:32:04.410
you think about a remote
operation on your vehicle,

00:32:04.410 --> 00:32:08.230
you need to have it happen
quickly rather than--

00:32:08.230 --> 00:32:11.670
and actually, you have to
balance the speed and user

00:32:11.670 --> 00:32:14.640
experience with the power
management that's going on

00:32:14.640 --> 00:32:15.630
in the vehicle as well.

00:32:18.720 --> 00:32:25.920
And as Mario mentioned,
as we move from 3G to 4G,

00:32:25.920 --> 00:32:32.310
even on into 5G, and potentially
even incorporating Wi-Fi,

00:32:32.310 --> 00:32:34.500
you need to have a
connectivity management

00:32:34.500 --> 00:32:38.340
system that can actually handle
all of those different modes.

00:32:44.860 --> 00:32:49.980
All right, and then one of the
other challenges that Bryan

00:32:49.980 --> 00:32:52.980
mentioned was security.

00:32:52.980 --> 00:32:55.410
And with regards
to security, you

00:32:55.410 --> 00:32:59.760
need to really take a
security domain approach.

00:32:59.760 --> 00:33:05.760
There are actually at least
eight major security domains

00:33:05.760 --> 00:33:08.190
in the connected vehicle space.

00:33:08.190 --> 00:33:10.050
And each one of those
security domains

00:33:10.050 --> 00:33:14.770
needs attention independently.

00:33:14.770 --> 00:33:17.840
And then you need to make sure
that you cover the full supply

00:33:17.840 --> 00:33:18.340
chain.

00:33:18.340 --> 00:33:23.660
If you think about it from
the time the car is created

00:33:23.660 --> 00:33:26.140
until the time the
car is crushed,

00:33:26.140 --> 00:33:30.640
there's so many different
parts of that lifecycle

00:33:30.640 --> 00:33:32.290
that come into play.

00:33:32.290 --> 00:33:36.610
And each one of those
stages in that lifecycle

00:33:36.610 --> 00:33:37.495
needs to be secured.

00:33:41.770 --> 00:33:44.160
And then relative to
connectivity, again,

00:33:44.160 --> 00:33:45.950
if you ignore connectivity--

00:33:49.060 --> 00:33:51.380
something that we
see, again, have

00:33:51.380 --> 00:33:55.970
seen from customers
in the past, that

00:33:55.970 --> 00:33:59.820
connectivity is a really
critical part of the security

00:33:59.820 --> 00:34:00.320
domain.

00:34:00.320 --> 00:34:04.070
Again, relative to
cellular, cellular

00:34:04.070 --> 00:34:08.480
was designed to be a many to
many type of communication

00:34:08.480 --> 00:34:10.460
mechanism if you think about it.

00:34:10.460 --> 00:34:14.659
So you have to apply
the security mechanisms

00:34:14.659 --> 00:34:20.270
to turn that many to many
into a secure, private network

00:34:20.270 --> 00:34:21.469
mechanism.

00:34:21.469 --> 00:34:26.389
And one of the most
famous hacks, actually,

00:34:26.389 --> 00:34:29.600
in connected vehicle
in the last few years

00:34:29.600 --> 00:34:34.699
was at least
exacerbated by the fact

00:34:34.699 --> 00:34:37.620
that the connectivity
security domain was ignored.

00:34:37.620 --> 00:34:38.540
So there was a--

00:34:38.540 --> 00:34:45.860
in that case, there was a hole
in the vehicle on the edge,

00:34:45.860 --> 00:34:48.920
which in itself
was bad, but what

00:34:48.920 --> 00:34:54.560
happened was the hole was
exposed via connectivity so

00:34:54.560 --> 00:35:00.080
that anybody who was on
that connectivity provider

00:35:00.080 --> 00:35:01.670
could actually access that hole.

00:35:07.170 --> 00:35:11.020
And it's key here actually to
assume that you're never done.

00:35:11.020 --> 00:35:15.610
You have to regularly
scan, and that's also

00:35:15.610 --> 00:35:17.575
why OTA updates are so critical.

00:35:21.950 --> 00:35:24.050
All right, and then
the last thing,

00:35:24.050 --> 00:35:30.350
I thought I'd spend a few
slides just talking about why

00:35:30.350 --> 00:35:33.410
Aeris is moving to GCP.

00:35:33.410 --> 00:35:36.500
And we're really proud,
as Mario mentioned,

00:35:36.500 --> 00:35:39.950
to become a partner of Google.

00:35:39.950 --> 00:35:43.010
And last year, like
perhaps some of you,

00:35:43.010 --> 00:35:46.820
I was sitting here
at Google Next,

00:35:46.820 --> 00:35:53.300
and we were contemplating
potentially moving to GCP.

00:35:53.300 --> 00:35:55.700
And ultimately, I just want
to share some of the reasons

00:35:55.700 --> 00:35:58.390
why we chose to make that move.

00:36:01.530 --> 00:36:08.620
So number one, we think of
Cloud 2.0 is really Kubernetes.

00:36:08.620 --> 00:36:11.550
If you think about
Cloud 1.0 being really

00:36:11.550 --> 00:36:13.830
about virtual
machines primarily,

00:36:13.830 --> 00:36:16.350
Cloud 2.0 is about Kubernetes.

00:36:16.350 --> 00:36:21.780
And of course, Google is creator
and the biggest contributor

00:36:21.780 --> 00:36:23.010
to Kubernetes.

00:36:23.010 --> 00:36:30.060
But in particular, GCP
has really a large lead

00:36:30.060 --> 00:36:32.280
on managed Kubernetes.

00:36:32.280 --> 00:36:35.520
So if you look really
across the cloud providers,

00:36:35.520 --> 00:36:38.250
and you do your own
evaluation like we did,

00:36:38.250 --> 00:36:41.790
you'll find that in
managed Kubernetes,

00:36:41.790 --> 00:36:44.160
Google actually has
a very strong lead.

00:36:44.160 --> 00:36:48.360
And the way we look at it is--

00:36:48.360 --> 00:36:52.560
and I love our
DevOps team, but if I

00:36:52.560 --> 00:36:57.120
have to make a choice of
hiring one more DevOps person

00:36:57.120 --> 00:37:01.680
or hiring a data scientist, I'd
rather hire the data scientist.

00:37:01.680 --> 00:37:05.580
And getting a good managed
Kubernetes infrastructure

00:37:05.580 --> 00:37:10.575
in place allows us to control
really are our DevOps workload.

00:37:13.980 --> 00:37:19.250
The second reason is about
really being customer

00:37:19.250 --> 00:37:20.240
and developer friendly.

00:37:23.390 --> 00:37:27.680
I go back to this
quote at the bottom,

00:37:27.680 --> 00:37:31.220
that's my quote actually
of Cloud 1.0 ROIs.

00:37:31.220 --> 00:37:32.810
If you think about
those ROIs, those

00:37:32.810 --> 00:37:36.860
were really about having your
own private data center where

00:37:36.860 --> 00:37:43.370
you had tens of usually,
operations, usually

00:37:43.370 --> 00:37:47.630
standard operations folks,
and the ROIs were relatively

00:37:47.630 --> 00:37:53.510
easy for kind of reducing a
large number of operations

00:37:53.510 --> 00:37:59.120
folks and moving those to be
DevOps folks in the cloud.

00:37:59.120 --> 00:38:02.510
But now, your CEO, or when you--

00:38:02.510 --> 00:38:05.400
when I say your CEO, I mean my
CEO is actually saying, hey,

00:38:05.400 --> 00:38:08.870
we're still spending too
much money in the cloud.

00:38:08.870 --> 00:38:12.690
What can we do that's
an improvement?

00:38:12.690 --> 00:38:16.490
And again, as we kind of
look around the industry

00:38:16.490 --> 00:38:20.630
at different cloud providers,
what we see out of GCP

00:38:20.630 --> 00:38:26.117
is that, just from cost
transparency and kind

00:38:26.117 --> 00:38:27.950
of optimization [? ease, ?]
there are really

00:38:27.950 --> 00:38:30.440
large advantages in GCP.

00:38:30.440 --> 00:38:38.710
And I think you can get to
some of these same capabilities

00:38:38.710 --> 00:38:40.560
in some of the other
cloud providers,

00:38:40.560 --> 00:38:42.920
but not nearly as easily.

00:38:42.920 --> 00:38:47.360
And for us, in our kind
of old cloud provider,

00:38:47.360 --> 00:38:50.750
we actually ended up
with 15 separate accounts

00:38:50.750 --> 00:38:55.790
in order to manage all
of the access controls

00:38:55.790 --> 00:38:59.540
and have the cost
visibility that we wanted.

00:38:59.540 --> 00:39:05.210
And what we see in GCP is
it's just tremendously easier

00:39:05.210 --> 00:39:09.620
to more automatically get
that visibility, the access

00:39:09.620 --> 00:39:12.050
control, and the
cost optimizations

00:39:12.050 --> 00:39:13.520
that we're trying to get to.

00:39:17.660 --> 00:39:22.870
And then the last big reason
is we do see this opportunity

00:39:22.870 --> 00:39:29.290
to actually collaborate with
Google and their customers

00:39:29.290 --> 00:39:33.850
on making the Internet of
Things less complex and easier

00:39:33.850 --> 00:39:34.640
for OEMs.

00:39:34.640 --> 00:39:39.880
We really see that nearly
every single OEM on the planet

00:39:39.880 --> 00:39:44.350
is on this journey from
unconnected product

00:39:44.350 --> 00:39:46.057
to connected service.

00:39:46.057 --> 00:39:47.890
And that's really
important, that difference

00:39:47.890 --> 00:39:50.950
between those words,
product and service.

00:39:50.950 --> 00:39:57.340
As Bryan mentioned, oftentimes
in the automotive space,

00:39:57.340 --> 00:40:01.990
the only interaction that the
actual automaker might have

00:40:01.990 --> 00:40:06.810
is maybe even just at the
point of the dealership.

00:40:06.810 --> 00:40:09.030
And that might be
the last interaction

00:40:09.030 --> 00:40:11.680
that they have with the
customer previously.

00:40:11.680 --> 00:40:15.420
So the fact that they can
have a connected vehicle,

00:40:15.420 --> 00:40:19.040
they can understand how
the consumer and the owner

00:40:19.040 --> 00:40:21.390
is actually using
the vehicle, they

00:40:21.390 --> 00:40:24.300
can have opportunities
to interact

00:40:24.300 --> 00:40:28.920
with the owner on a
regular basis, really,

00:40:28.920 --> 00:40:31.620
it's a game-changer
in the business,

00:40:31.620 --> 00:40:33.165
and in the business
of many OEMs.

00:40:36.460 --> 00:40:41.650
So for us, we see some of the
areas that we're working with,

00:40:41.650 --> 00:40:45.910
provisioning at IoT
scale in a secure way,

00:40:45.910 --> 00:40:49.200
seamless connectivity and
OTA updates of edge devices.

00:40:49.200 --> 00:40:50.800
I've mentioned a
few times about why

00:40:50.800 --> 00:40:53.530
that's important in the space.

00:40:53.530 --> 00:40:56.110
The advanced remote
operations services.

00:40:56.110 --> 00:41:00.250
If you think about, especially
in the moving things

00:41:00.250 --> 00:41:08.740
area, the fact that you
have MQTT and IoT core

00:41:08.740 --> 00:41:11.770
and some of those
things, those are great.

00:41:11.770 --> 00:41:16.570
But it doesn't make sense
for every single OEM

00:41:16.570 --> 00:41:19.990
to reinvent how remote
operations are done.

00:41:19.990 --> 00:41:23.920
A lot of that needs to be more
standardized and sharable and

00:41:23.920 --> 00:41:27.350
leverageable.

00:41:27.350 --> 00:41:31.640
And then the automotive
grade edge computing.

00:41:31.640 --> 00:41:37.700
If you think about a
future where, again, the--

00:41:37.700 --> 00:41:43.250
if you think about Web 1.0
to Web 2.0, in Web 1.0,

00:41:43.250 --> 00:41:47.360
you also had more of
very rudimentary kind

00:41:47.360 --> 00:41:51.420
of logic getting pushed to
the edge to your browser,

00:41:51.420 --> 00:41:55.970
and then kind of interacting
over rudimentary APIs

00:41:55.970 --> 00:41:57.380
back to the platform.

00:41:57.380 --> 00:42:01.310
That's a similar thing that's
going to happen as in the edge

00:42:01.310 --> 00:42:02.480
computing area.

00:42:02.480 --> 00:42:06.950
So that we have the
edge computing logic

00:42:06.950 --> 00:42:13.820
is more of a adaptable,
